ERPNext 16 is installed under /usr/lib/erpnext with sites and
uploaded files in /var/lib/erpnext/sites. The web service listens
on 127.0.0.1:8000. Realtime uses 127.0.0.1:9000. MariaDB is local.
Redis is three private instances on Unix sockets:
redis@erpnext-cache, redis@erpnext-queue and redis@erpnext-socketio.

This package bundles Frappe 16.35.0. Python dependencies that are
not already in the distribution are separate system packages.
The interpreter is the system Python. psycopg2-binary and pypdfium2 are not
included: PostgreSQL uses the system psycopg2 module, and PDF page
rasterization that needs pdfium is unavailable.

1. Start MariaDB and the private Redis instances:

   systemctl enable --now mariadb redis@erpnext-cache redis@erpnext-queue redis@erpnext-socketio

2. Create the first site. MariaDB root on this system authenticates
   through the local socket, so run the command as root. Replace the
   passwords. The site name is the host name you will open in the
   browser, or "localhost" if you only use the forwarded port.

   erpnext new-site localhost \
     --admin-password 'choose-one' \
     --mariadb-root-username root \
     --db-root-password '' \
     --install-app erpnext

   If the database root user has a password, put it in
   --db-root-password.

3. Start the application:

   systemctl enable --now erpnext erpnext-worker erpnext-scheduler erpnext-socketio

4. Open http://localhost:8000 and log in as Administrator with the
   admin password from step 2.

To put nginx in front, install erpnext-nginx and enable the site:

   ln -s ../sites-available/erpnext.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/erpnext.conf
   systemctl reload nginx

The nginx site answers erpnext.* and proxies to the local ports.
